	(text_box "PASS FETS: DRIVE THEM FULLY ON OR OFF, NOT IN BETWEEN.\nRound 28.  The three FETs share one gate voltage and DMN6068SE Vth is 1.0-3.0 V\n(datasheet), so at any intermediate gate voltage which bank conducts is a\nlottery; worse, at half current a FET's own heating (Vth tempco x RthJA) has\nloop gain above 1, so a bank snaps to full-on.  Linear sharing needs an op-amp\ncurrent sink per FET from the 15 V rail - a Rev B change.  On this board the\nsoftware drives the DAC to 0 or full scale with a duty cycle of 30 s or more\nagainst a ~5 min board time constant: at Vgs = 3.3 V every DMN6068SE carries the\n214 mA its 70 ohm bank allows, dissipates ~3 mW instead of the 0.8 W a linear\nhalf-current point would, and all 24 heaters switch together.  Three FETs stay\nfor the local drain nets.  Drive comes from the MCP4725 on the housekeeping\nsheet."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 403.86 347.98 0)
		(size 121.92 34.29)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 255 245 210 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4 1.4)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "0e6f3817-da97-9811-1d57-f1d90ae8a147")
	)
	(text_box "TWENTY-FOUR HEATERS, AND THREE PASS FETS.\n24 x 560R in parallel is 23.3 ohm, so 9.6 W full on at 643 mA and 400 mW per\nresistor - 53 percent of a 2010's 0.75 W rating.  Thirteen of them sit INSIDE\nthe channel tiles, one per channel, and eleven ring the array.  They are spread\nacross the board because what hurts is not the mean temperature but the\nGRADIENT: a gradient is what makes channel 3 drift differently from channel 11.\nThe four corner TMP1075s are what tell you whether the spread is good enough."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 274.32 347.98 0)
		(size 121.92 21.59)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 220 235 255 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4 1.4)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "34a5b1e4-413d-bfa0-5bae-9d0ceb47a262")
	)
	(text_box "LINEAR, NOT PWM - AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.\nWe are making heat on purpose, so dissipation in the pass element is not waste.\nA linear element means there is no switching edge anywhere on a board doing\npicosecond timing; a PWM heater is a few hundred mA of square wave circulating\nsomewhere, which is exactly what the helper-tree work is trying to keep off the\nground plane.  Round 28 qualifies this: the FETs are driven fully on or off with\na duty cycle of 30 s or more (see PASS FET), which is not PWM in that sense -\none ramped edge every half minute, and nothing in the measurement band."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 144.78 347.98 0)
		(size 121.92 24.13)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 220 235 255 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4 1.4)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "4ff1a50b-3e2a-27cc-f82b-4e7869726e74")
	)
	(text_box "STUDENT-PROOFING THE 5 V INPUT.\nBench supplies get set wrong.  Three things, in order: each source has its own\nSERIES SCHOTTKY, so reversed leads conduct nothing and the two sources cannot\nback-feed each other; a 500 mA PPTC, which RESETS ITSELF - nobody has to find\nthe bag of spare fuses; and a 5.6 V 5 W Zener, which is the part that saves the\nboard if 12 V lands on the 5 V posts.\nThe Zener is what makes the fuse work: it clamps the rail near 6.1 V - under the\nTPS7A2033's 6.5 V absolute maximum - and draws the amp that trips the PPTC in\nabout 100 ms.  It is the deliberate sacrificial part, and it is a 16-cent SMB on\nthe board edge.\nWhat is NOT protected: sustained reverse voltage beyond the SS34's 40 V, and\nanything applied directly to an SMA or the header pins.\nTHE RAILS CANNOT COME UP WITHOUT THE HOST (round 28).  PYNQ-Z2 user manual table\n15: an unpowered Zynq I/O pin is absolute-max 0.55 V, and a bench-powered board\nwith the PYNQ off would drive 3.3 V into all 13 beats, 12 pps returns and the\nhelper clock - 14 mA a pin through the PYNQ's 200 R, survivable but out of spec\nevery second it lasts, and rounds 21-27 had only silkscreen against it.  So both\nLDO enables now come from +5V_HDR, the HEADER side of the OR-ing diode, through\nR2025: with the host off, LDO_EN is held low by each TPS7A20's own 500k pulldown\nand neither 3.3 V rail exists.  The bench posts still work with the PYNQ also\non.  For a board that will NEVER sit on a host (a USB-I2C dongle on the debug\nheader instead), fit R2026, a 0R, which ties LDO_EN to the LDO input."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 403.86 144.78 0)
		(size 121.92 62.23)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 255 220 220 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4 1.4)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "5211060f-ab71-dec1-ec7c-a7f531e10b40")
	)
	(text_box "CURRENT BUDGET, ~100 mA TOTAL.\n14 x 74LVC1GU04 self-biased in the linear region ~3 mA each = 42 mA;  26 x\n74LVC1G80 at 10 MHz ~1 mA each = 26 mA;  5 x 74LVC1G34 fan-out ~2 mA each = 10\nmA; 12 x 74LVC1G17 at 1 Hz ~ 1 mA total;  Si5351 ~25 mA.  The linear-region\ncurrent of a self-biased unbuffered inverter is not a datasheet number - measure\nit on the first board."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 403.86 100.33 0)
		(size 121.92 17.78)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 255 245 210 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4 1.4)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "72ec07be-59e6-c145-af74-bd28c95e85e9")
	)
	(text_box "ONE SHARED RAIL FOR EVERY THRESHOLD-SETTING GATE.\n+3V3_A feeds all 14 squarers, all 26 flip-flops, all 12 Schmitts and the whole\nhelper fan-out.  A gate's switching threshold moves with its supply, so on a\nshared rail every channel's threshold moves TOGETHER - common mode, and it\ncancels in the differences we actually compute BETWEEN CHANNELS OF EQUAL SLEW.\nRound 28: dt = dV / slew, and the inventory's slews span 23 mV/ns (FE-5680A,\n0.72 Vpp sine) to ~1600 mV/ns (ZED-F9T CMOS), so 1 mV of common rail shift is 44\nps on one and 0.6 ps on the other, and 43 ps of it does NOT cancel between them.\nThat residual is the analog floor for the sine sources and the reason the board\nis heated and the rail is a 7 uV LDO.  Fourteen separate regulators would still\nbe worse: they would add fourteen independent wanders on top."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 15.24 144.78 0)
		(size 121.92 31.75)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 220 235 255 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4 1.4)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "9bc45042-0687-5148-8dda-b3b5d7a7bef4")
	)
	(text_box "WHY THE TPS7A2033.\n7 uV RMS output noise (10 Hz - 100 kHz, 300 mA; 10 uV at 1 mA), 300 mA, and 52\n000 in stock at JLCPCB.  The obvious alternative, the ADP151, is 9 uV and 200 mA\nand is not stocked there at all - only re-marked house-brand parts are.\nThe low noise needs NO external part: the reference filter is a 0.1 Hz first-\norder RC on the die, and the soft-start is internal too.  Pin 4 of the SOT-23-5\nis a true no-connect.  Leave it open."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 274.32 144.78 0)
		(size 121.92 21.59)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 255 245 210 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4 1.4)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "a99580a6-5b2a-8470-ff89-e9526f032491")
	)
	(text_box "WHY A SECOND RAIL AT ALL.\n+3V3_D carries only the Si5351, the TCXO and the I2C housekeeping parts.  None\nof them sets a comparison threshold, and they draw the lumpiest current on the\nboard. Splitting them off halves the dissipation in each SOT-23 package - about\n120 mW and 50 mW instead of 170 mW in one - which matters on a board whose whole\npurpose is thermal stability."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 144.78 144.78 0)
		(size 121.92 17.78)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 220 235 255 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4 1.4)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "adfe4436-bbe2-0582-04e7-6b5a8ecb0e88")
	)
	(text_box "12 V ON ITS OWN JACK, HEATER ONLY.\nIt never touches the 3.3 V rails or the FPGA.  The heater's 9.6 W comes from the\n15 V, not from the PYNQ-Z2's 5 V rail - which matters because it is twice what\nthe rest of this board draws.  Full on is 643 mA, so a 1 A supply is plenty and\nthe silkscreen says so.  A RESETTABLE 1.1 A fuse, a series Schottky for reverse\npolarity and an 18 V TVS mean a wrong supply blows nothing; 18 V because a 15 V\nrail at +5 percent is 15.75 and a 16 V part would have sat 250 mV from\nconducting.\nSILKSCREEN: 15 V DC, 1 A MIN - HEATER ONLY - CENTRE POSITIVE."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 15.24 347.98 0)
		(size 121.92 26.67)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type default)
		)
		(fill
			(type color)
			(color 255 220 220 1)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.4 1.4)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "e4e0d58f-c41d-9a67-a2b2-b55c56934f9a")
	)
